{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794460317,"nonce":"FGJBEnTSjkzU4mrE3klRg/Jpypw=","title":"x","message":"Document `kernel.yama.ptrace_scope \u003e= 1` as recommended hardening for nodes that run Step Pods, in the operator docs (Helm README / install page). Do not check it in code.\n\nWhy it's only a recommendation:\n- Yama checks only attach-mode access (`ptrace`, `/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/mem`). It doesn't check read-mode `/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/root` or `/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/environ`. What keeps a Dockerfile RUN out of buildkitd and buildctl is the kernel's capability rule, at any scope (6f54105, measured 2026-11-12).\n- In the one case where the capability rule fails (`security.insecure`), the leak path is read-mode, so scope 1 wouldn't stop it.\n- AL2023, the EKS default, ships with scope 0, and Scarab can only read the setting, not change it. Refusing to run at 0 would break every default EKS cluster for little protection.\n\nCost to operators at scope 1: `strace -p`, `gdb -p` and `py-spy --pid` against non-child processes need CAP_SYS_PTRACE. `strace cmd` and `gdb ./prog` still work. Ubuntu ships with scope 1.\n\nHow to set it on AL2023 / EKS: a sysctl drop-in through the node's userData (Karpenter EC2NodeClass or launch template).","files":null}]}